---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site.config';
import { getPosts } from '../lib/posts';

const posts = await getPosts();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`About ${SITE.title} and how the notes are organised.`}
  lang="en"
>
  <div class="container page">
    <div class="reading">
      <header class="page-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">{SITE.title}</h1>
        <p class="page-lead">{SITE.description}</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          This site is a personal knowledge base: part blog, part notebook. It
          keeps course notes, tool setup steps, and reading summaries in one
          place, and tries to make all of it easy to find again.
        </p>

        <h2>How it is organised</h2>
        <p>
          Two structures work together. A <strong>category</strong> describes the
          broad area a note belongs to — tools, AI, and whatever comes next. A
          <strong>tag</strong> is a narrow, reusable keyword that can cross those
          boundaries. Categories keep the archive tidy; tags let a thread run
          through it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every post is also indexed for full-text search. Press
          <kbd>⌘</kbd> + <kbd>K</kbd> anywhere to jump to search, or browse the
          <a href="/posts">archive</a> and <a href="/tags">tag index</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>What you will find</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Tools</strong> — environment setup, Git, Docker, CUDA, and
            the commands worth remembering.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>AI</strong> — Transformer architecture and deep learning
            study notes, written to explain the ideas to myself.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>References</strong> — links and summaries that deserve a
            second visit.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Colophon</h2>
        <p>
          Built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> and Markdown,
          rendered as static HTML with no client-side framework. Typography uses
          system fonts, so pages stay fast and readable. Math is typeset with
          KaTeX at build time. Posts live in
          <code>src/content/posts</code>; adding a file is enough to publish.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>
            A note is only useful if you can find it again. Everything here is
            designed around that constraint.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
          There are currently {posts.length} posts. You can subscribe via
          <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>
          {SITE.email && (
            <>
              {' '}or write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
            </>
          )}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
